    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d290">

                <pb facs="0247" n="197" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg197"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.77/158</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Francis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Petrograd</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1917-08-25T19:00:00+03:31"><hi rendition="#italic">August 25,
                                1917, 7 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received August 28, 8.10
                        p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1664. Stevens,
                    Miller, Darling finally started
                    special car last evening to put in operation their recommendations on the
                    Siberian Railway.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d290-f1"/> I personally saw them off.
                    This accomplished after my repeated interviews with Minister for Foreign Affairs
                    and two interviews with Kerensky on the
                    subject and informal conferences between commission and officials of the
                    Department of Ways of Communication. Stevens although sometimes discouraged and threatening
                    abandonment of the (omission) left in good spirits. Kerensky told me personally that the American Railway Commission
                    recommendations be made effective on the Siberian Railway and Council of
                    Ministers appointed Ustrugov, Assistant
                    Minister of Ways of Communication, to make same operative. He promised <pb
                        facs="0248" n="198" xml:id="pg198"/> to accompany commission but failed.
                    Promised to leave to-night or to-morrow and join commission. I again visited
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs told him would consider Ustrugov’s failure to join commission almost a breach of
                    international courtesy. Received assurances would be no failure.</p>
                <p>Darling report concerning Murman Railway rather encouraging, am looking after
                    that also as transportation essential factor in situation. Please show Willard.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Francis</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d290-f1" n="1" xml:id="d290-f1-content">
                <p>Henry Miller and W. F. Darling were
                    members of the Advisory Commission of Railway Experts to Russia, of which
                        John F. Stevens was chairman.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
